# Per-agent secrets — backoffice
#
# Copy to agents/backoffice/.env and fill in. That file is gitignored (the root
# .gitignore's ".env" pattern matches at any depth) and mounted read-only at
# /opt/data/profiles/backoffice/.env.
#
#   cp agents/backoffice/.env.example agents/backoffice/.env
#
# DO THIS BEFORE "docker compose up -d". The mount uses the long form with
# create_host_path: false precisely so a missing file stops the stack with
# "bind source path does not exist" — with the short form Docker would silently
# create a root-owned *directory* here and the agent would read no secrets at
# all. Same reasoning as the ${TAILSCALE_IP:?} guard in docker-compose.yml.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE, AND WHAT DOES NOT
#
# Container-wide secrets stay in the root .env and reach the agent through the
# compose "environment:" list. Put a key here only when THIS agent must hold a
# different one from every other agent — which is the whole reason per-agent
# credentials exist: access scoping that prompting cannot enforce (plan §0).

# --- Per-agent Notion access ---
# Each agent gets its own Notion integration, scoped in Notion itself to the
# databases that agent may read. That scoping is the access boundary; the
# persona is not.
#
# Left commented on purpose. Nothing in this deployment consumes it yet — no
# Notion sync, no crm-mcp (plan §4). Uncommenting it hands the agent a live
# Notion credential before the screening layer exists, which is acceptable only
# while the surface stays Tailscale-only and never once Slack lands. See
# gap-register G1.
#
# TWO NAMES, ONE SECRET. The skill declares NOTION_API_KEY as its prerequisite
# and uses it in every curl example; the `ntn` CLI it drives reads
# NOTION_API_TOKEN. Both are genuinely needed, so both are set here — the
# alternative, patching the variable name inside the bundled skill at image
# build time, buys one fewer line at the cost of owning a patch against an
# upstream file forever. Not worth it.
#
# Set only the key and the failure is quiet in the worst way: the skill reports
# itself correctly configured while `ntn` reports "API token is invalid".
#
# The second line references the first rather than repeating the value: Hermes
# loads this file with python-dotenv, which interpolates ${VAR}, so a rotated
# key changes in exactly one place. (If `ntn` ever rejects a key you know is
# good, that is the thing to check — paste the literal value to rule it out.)
#
# The CLI's other requirement, NOTION_KEYRING=0, is NOT here: it is a fact about
# running headless rather than an agent secret, so it lives in
# docker/Dockerfile alongside the CLI itself.
#NOTION_API_KEY=ntn_...
#NOTION_API_TOKEN=${NOTION_API_KEY}

# --- Slack ---
# The three credentials from the app defined by slack-manifest.yaml. Fill these
# in and the agent joins its channel on the next deploy; leave them unset and
# the Slack surface simply does not come up.
#
# THEY BELONG HERE, NOT IN THE ROOT .env. Plan §6.1 says to put them in the
# root .env and add them to the compose "environment:" list, but that predates
# this per-agent mount. The compose environment is CONTAINER-WIDE: a second
# persona added later would silently inherit the backoffice bot's credentials
# and be able to post as it. One Slack app per agent, one token set per agent,
# scoped in Slack itself — the rule at the top of this file.
#
# Socket Mode is outbound, so none of this needs a port or a compose change.
#
# Verify before deploying:  ./scripts/slack-preflight.sh check
#
# Install App → Bot User OAuth Token
#SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
# Basic Information → App-Level Tokens (created when you enable Socket Mode,
# needs the connections:write scope)
#SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...
# Basic Information → App Credentials → Signing Secret
#SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=...

# --- Per-agent chat model key ---
# Only if this agent needs a different provider or key from the container-wide
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY. Leave unset to inherit that one.
#OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
